    <string name="mod_ct">CT scan</string>
    <string name="mod_ct_short">Cross-sections using X-rays</string>
    <string name="mod_ct_long">A CT scanner uses X-rays to make thin cross-sections of the body. The grey values are Hounsfield units (HU): air is −1000, water 0 and bone from several hundred to over 1000. That is why the Window/Level presets in this app use HU.</string>
    <string name="mod_mr">MRI</string>
    <string name="mod_mr_short">Magnetic field, lots of detail</string>
    <string name="mod_mr_long">MRI uses a strong magnetic field and radio waves, without X-rays. It shows soft tissue such as the brain, muscles and joints in great detail. The grey values have no fixed scale as with CT.</string>
    <string name="mod_xa">Angiography</string>
    <string name="mod_xa_short">Blood vessels, as a film</string>
    <string name="mod_xa_long">In angiography, blood vessels are made visible with contrast fluid while X-ray images are taken, often as a short film (cine). The sample scans in this app show the coronary arteries.</string>
    <string name="mod_us">Ultrasound</string>
    <string name="mod_us_short">Sound waves, no radiation</string>
    <string name="mod_us_long">Ultrasound makes images with sound waves, without radiation. The image is live; a DICOM file holds single images or short clips.</string>
    <string name="mod_cr">X-ray</string>
    <string name="mod_cr_short">A single projection image</string>
    <string name="mod_cr_long">A plain X-ray (CR or DX) is a single projection image, for example of bones or lungs. Anything that blocks the radiation, such as bone, appears light.</string>
    <string name="mod_mg">Mammography</string>
    <string name="mod_mg_short">X-ray of the breast</string>
    <string name="mod_mg_long">A mammogram is an X-ray of the breast with a low radiation dose. The images are large and very detailed.</string>
